WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.340 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
Hey, it's Andrea here, founder of
LeakLine. And I'm building LeakLine for

00:06.340 --> 00:10.720 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
high-ticket businesses that already have
sales coming in, but are losing money

00:10.720 --> 00:16.220 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
after the sale through things like failed
payments, overdue balances, refund risk,

00:16.800 --> 00:23.020 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
chargebacks or broken payment plans. The
idea is simple. Before you go and spend

00:23.020 --> 00:29.000 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
more money on leads or more sales calls,
LeakLine helps you recover revenue that

00:29.000 --> 00:35.340 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
should already be yours. So this is mainly
for operators, sales ops people, founders

00:35.340 --> 00:41.120 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
or teams running online coaching, info
products, agency models or high-ticket

00:41.120 --> 00:46.900 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
offers. So if you've got payment plans,
collections, failed payments, refund

00:46.900 --> 00:50.780 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
requests, or customers simply just
slipping through the cracks after they

00:50.780 --> 00:57.200 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
buy, this is exactly the kind of thing
that I'm building for. Now, I've worked in

00:57.200 --> 01:02.060 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
high-ticket sales as a closer, and one
thing I've noticed is that a lot of

01:02.060 --> 01:08.400 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
attention goes into closing deals, but not
enough goes into actually protecting the

01:08.400 --> 01:14.520 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
revenue after the close. So a deal may be
marked as won, but then payments fail,

01:15.080 --> 01:19.940 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
follow-up gets inconsistent, and no one
knows who owns the recovery. Revenue

01:19.940 --> 01:26.980 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
quietly leaks out. So inside LeakLine, the
first thing you see is a revenue leak view

01:26.980 --> 01:32.940 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
in the Revenue Recovery Command Center.
This gives you visibility on where money

01:32.940 --> 01:37.740 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
is at risk. So instead of just seeing
sales numbers, you can see things like

01:37.740 --> 01:43.560 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
failed payments, overdue cash, refund risk
or accounts that just simply need follow-up.

01:43.560 --> 01:50.180 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now, the goal here isn't just reporting.
It's to show you what actually

01:50.180 --> 01:57.140 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
needs attention. When I click into a
recovery case, this is where it gets more

01:57.140 --> 02:04.600 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
useful. You can see the customer or deal,
what the issue is, the payment processor

02:04.600 --> 02:09.460 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
that was used to process the payment, how
long they're overdue, how much revenue is

02:09.460 --> 02:16.260 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
at risk, and what needs to happen next. So
for example, if someone's payment failed

02:16.260 --> 02:20.600 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
or they've missed the payment plan
instalment, LeakLine turns that into a

02:20.600 --> 02:25.140 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
clear recovery task instead of it just
sitting somewhere in your payment

02:25.140 --> 02:32.140 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
processor, your Slack, a CRM or just
someone's memory. And this is the part I

02:32.140 --> 02:38.860 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
care about the most. LeakLine doesn't just
say you have a revenue leak. It helps

02:38.860 --> 02:45.220 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
create the next action. And that could be
follow-up with the customer, assigning it

02:45.220 --> 02:50.440 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
to someone on the team, checking whether
or not it's a refund risk, or just simply

02:50.440 --> 02:56.440 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
moving the case forward once the case is
recovered. Now, the goal is to make

02:56.440 --> 03:02.520 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
payment recovery more operational, so the
team knows what to chase, who owns it, and

03:02.520 --> 03:07.900 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
what's already been handled. Now, right
now, I'm looking for a few high-ticket

03:07.900 --> 03:12.060 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
businesses to test this with. I'm not
pretending this is some fully polished

03:12.060 --> 03:17.780 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
enterprise platform yet. I'm trying to
work with real operators, understand where

03:17.780 --> 03:22.460 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
revenue is leaking, and see whether
LeakLine can actually help recover money

03:22.460 --> 03:28.100 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
that would otherwise be missed. So if you
run a high-ticket offer and you've dealt

03:28.100 --> 03:35.540 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
with failed payments, refunds, overdue
balances, or payment plan follow-up, I'd

03:35.540 --> 03:40.660 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
love to get your feedback. You can either
book in and request a free revenue

03:40.660 --> 03:45.760 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
recovery assessment or get in contact with
us in the contact section at the bottom of

03:45.760 --> 03:50.020 line:82% position:50% align:center size:88%
this page, and I'll show you how this
could apply directly to your setup.
